  standards:[
  {code:"A.FGR.2", title:"Linear functions and arithmetic sequences", unit:"Unit 1",
   text:"Construct and interpret arithmetic sequences as functions, algebraically and graphically, to model and explain real-life phenomena. Use formal notation to represent linear functions and the key characteristics of graphs of linear functions, and informally compare linear and non-linear functions using parent graphs.",
   keys:[
    "An <b>arithmetic sequence</b> adds the same amount (the common difference <i>d</i>) every term. Recursive: <span class='math'>a<sub>n</sub> = a<sub>n−1</sub> + d</span>. Explicit: <span class='math'>a<sub>n</sub> = a<sub>1</sub> + (n − 1)d</span>. Both are on your formula sheet.",
    "An arithmetic sequence is a linear function whose domain is whole-number term positions. The sequence <span class='math'>a<sub>1</sub> = 7, a<sub>n</sub> = a<sub>n−1</sub> + 2</span>, the explicit rule <span class='math'>a<sub>n</sub> = 2(n − 1) + 7</span>, and <span class='math'>f(x) = 2x + 5</span> (for natural numbers <i>x</i>) all list 7, 9, 11, 13, ...",
    "In <span class='math'>f(x) = mx + b</span>, <i>m</i> is the slope (rate of change, rise over run) and <i>b</i> is the y-intercept (the starting value when <i>x</i> = 0). Slope formula: <span class='math'>m = (y<sub>2</sub> − y<sub>1</sub>) / (x<sub>2</sub> − x<sub>1</sub>)</span>.",
    "<b>Function notation:</b> <span class='math'>f(3) = 200</span> means the input is 3 and the output is 200. To evaluate, substitute the input for <i>x</i>.",
