    {q:"A beautician's profit after c clients is P(c) = 50c − 200. Identify and interpret the key characteristics.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 08A2024 · DOK 3",
     steps:["Slope 50: she earns $50 more profit for each client.","y-intercept −200: before any clients, she is $200 in debt from the startup money she borrowed.","Domain: whole numbers of clients, <span class='math'>{c | c = 0, 1, 2, 3, ...}</span>. Range: <span class='math'>{P | P = −200, −150, −100, ...}</span>.","The function is increasing over its whole domain. Set <span class='math'>50c − 200 = 0</span> → c = 4. Profit is negative for c &lt; 4, zero at c = 4 (break-even), and positive for c &gt; 4."]},
    {q:"Revenue (in thousands) is f(x) = 2x + 3 for the first 12 months. Give the domain and range in interval notation.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 04A2024 · DOK 2",
     steps:["Domain: months 0 through 12 → <b>[0, 12]</b>.","Range: smallest output f(0) = 3, largest f(12) = 2(12) + 3 = 27 → <b>[3, 27]</b>, which is $3,000 to $27,000."]}],
   mis:[
    {t:"Using n instead of (n − 1).", d:"For −13, −9, −5, ... the 20th term is −13 + 19(4) = 63. Multiplying by 20 gives 67, which is the 21st term."},
    {t:"Using rise alone as the slope.", d:"From (0, −1) to (2, 0), y rises 1 while x runs 2. The slope is 1/2, not 1."},
    {t:"Mixing up the x-intercept and y-intercept.", d:"The y-intercept is where the graph crosses the y-axis (x = 0). The x-intercept is where it crosses the x-axis (y = 0)."},
    {t:"Swapping the rate and the starting value.", d:"In s(x) = 3.10 + 0.05x, the constant 3.10 is the starting value and 0.05 (multiplied by x) is the change per year."},
    {t:"Allowing fractions for things you count.", d:"Fish, clients, and engines come in whole numbers, so the domain is integers. Time, mass, and temperature can take any value in a range."}],
   items:["S02","IB03","IB06","IB07B","IB01","S16","S10","S11","S12"]},

  {code:"A.FGR.7", title:"Quadratic functions and their graphs", unit:"Unit 4",
   text:"Construct and interpret quadratic functions from data points to model and explain real-life phenomena; describe key characteristics of the graph of a quadratic function to explain a mathematically applicable situation for which the graph serves as a model.",
   keys:[
    "Three forms (on your formula sheet), each revealing something different. <b>Standard</b> <span class='math'>y = ax<sup>2</sup> + bx + c</span> shows the y-intercept <i>c</i>. <b>Vertex</b> <span class='math'>y = a(x − h)<sup>2</sup> + k</span> shows the vertex (h, k), the maximum or minimum. <b>Factored</b> <span class='math'>y = a(x − p)(x − q)</span> shows the zeros (x-intercepts) <i>p</i> and <i>q</i>.",
    "If <i>a</i> &gt; 0 the parabola opens up and the vertex is a minimum. If <i>a</i> &lt; 0 it opens down and the vertex is a maximum. The axis of symmetry is the vertical line x = h, halfway between the zeros. From standard form, <span class='math'>h = −b / (2a)</span>.",
    "To move from standard to vertex form, complete the square. Whatever you add inside, you must also subtract: <span class='math'>x<sup>2</sup> − 8x + 15 = (x<sup>2</sup> − 8x + 16) − 16 + 15 = (x − 4)<sup>2</sup> − 1</span>.",
    "Transformations of <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>): <span class='math'>f(x) + k</span> shifts up k (down if k is negative). <span class='math'>k·f(x)</span> stretches vertically (and flips over the x-axis if k is negative). <span class='math'>f(kx)</span> squeezes or stretches horizontally. <span class='math'>f(x + k)</span> shifts left k (right if k is negative).",
    "<b>Average rate of change</b> = change in y ÷ change in x between two points. For a linear function it is the same on every interval. For a quadratic it changes from interval to interval.",
    "In context, the domain usually starts at 0 (time can't be negative) and ends when the object hits the ground. A bird drops a stick from a nest 36 feet up: <span class='math'>f(x) = −16x<sup>2</sup> + 36</span>. It lands when <span class='math'>16x<sup>2</sup> = 36</span>, x = 1.5, so the domain is <span class='math'>0 ≤ x ≤ 1.5</span> and the range is <span class='math'>0 ≤ y ≤ 36</span>."],
   examples:[
    {q:"A free throw follows y = −2x<sup>2</sup> + 8x + 3. Find the vertex, the release height, and where the ball's path increases and decreases.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 24A2024, Parts A to C · DOK 3",
     steps:["<span class='math'>h = −b/(2a) = −8 / (2·(−2)) = 2</span>. Then <span class='math'>y = −2(2)<sup>2</sup> + 8(2) + 3 = −8 + 16 + 3 = 11</span>. Vertex <b>(2, 11)</b>: the ball's maximum height is 11 ft.","Release height is the y-intercept: when x = 0, y = <b>3</b>. On the graph this is where the path starts on the y-axis.","Since a = −2 opens down, the path is increasing for x &lt; 2 (from the release) and decreasing for x &gt; 2."]},
    {q:"A toy rocket's height is h = −16t<sup>2</sup> + 64t + 36. What is the appropriate domain?", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 33A2024 · DOK 1",
     steps:["Time starts at launch, so t ≥ 0.","The rocket lands when h = 0: <span class='math'>−16t<sup>2</sup> + 64t + 36 = 0</span>. Divide by −4: <span class='math'>4t<sup>2</sup> − 16t − 9 = 0</span> → <span class='math'>(2t − 9)(2t + 1) = 0</span>.","t = 4.5 or t = −0.5. Negative time doesn't fit the context, so the domain is <b>0 ≤ t ≤ 4.5</b> seconds."]},
    {q:"A tennis ball's height is f(t) = −16t<sup>2</sup> + 400. Find the initial height, the landing time, and compare average rates of change.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 37A2024 · DOK 3",
     steps:["Initial height: f(0) = <b>400 ft</b>.","Lands when <span class='math'>16t<sup>2</sup> = 400</span> → t<sup>2</sup> = 25 → <b>t = 5 s</b>.","f(0) = 400, f(1) = 384, f(2) = 336. Average rate of change from 0 to 1: (384 − 400)/1 = −16 ft/s. From 1 to 2: (336 − 384)/1 = −48 ft/s. The ball falls faster each second, so the rate is not constant like a linear function's."]}],
   mis:[
    {t:"Completing the square without balancing.", d:"Adding 16 to make (x − 4)<sup>2</sup> means you must subtract 16 too. x<sup>2</sup> − 8x + 15 becomes (x − 4)<sup>2</sup> − 1, not (x − 4)<sup>2</sup> + 15."},
    {t:"Thinking factored form shows the minimum.", d:"(x − 3)(x − 5) shows the zeros 3 and 5. The minimum is at the vertex, halfway between them at x = 4."},
    {t:"Getting the sign of h or the zeros backward.", d:"In (x − 4)<sup>2</sup>, the vertex is at x = +4. In (x − 3)(x − 5) the zeros are +3 and +5."},
    {t:"Reading the input from inside the parentheses.", d:"In g(4) = 0.01(4 − 1)<sup>2</sup> + 2.95, the input is 4 (year 4). The (x − 1) is part of the rule, not the year."},
    {t:"Confusing maximum height with maximum time.", d:"The vertex is the highest point. The ball is in the air until it lands at the positive zero."}],
   items:["S01","S13","S17","IB38","IB36","IB34","IB35"]},

  {code:"A.FGR.9", title:"Exponential functions and geometric sequences", unit:"Unit 6",
   text:"Construct and analyze the graph of an exponential function to explain a mathematically applicable situation for which the graph serves as a model; compare exponential with linear and quadratic functions.",
   keys:[
    "In <span class='math'>f(x) = a·b<sup>x</sup></span>, <i>a</i> is the initial value (the y-intercept, when x = 0) and <i>b</i> is the growth factor: what you multiply by each time x goes up 1.",
    "If b &gt; 1 the function grows; if 0 &lt; b &lt; 1 it decays. A growth rate r means b = 1 + r (5% growth → 1.05). A decay rate r means b = 1 − r (5% decay → 0.95).",
    "From a graph or table, find <i>a</i> at x = 0, then find <i>b</i> by dividing one output by the output before it: f(1) ÷ f(0).",
    "A <b>geometric sequence</b> multiplies by the same ratio <i>r</i>. Recursive: <span class='math'>a<sub>n</sub> = r·a<sub>n−1</sub></span>. Explicit: <span class='math'>a<sub>n</sub> = a<sub>1</sub>·r<sup>n−1</sup></span>. The sequence <span class='math'>a<sub>1</sub> = 8, a<sub>n</sub> = 2a<sub>n−1</sub></span>, the rule <span class='math'>s<sub>n</sub> = 8(2)<sup>n−1</sup></span>, and <span class='math'>f(x) = 4(2)<sup>x</sup></span> (natural x) all list 8, 16, 32, ...",
    "<span class='math'>f(x) + k</span> shifts the graph and its horizontal asymptote up or down k. <span class='math'>k·f(x)</span> multiplies every output by k, so the initial value becomes k·a.",
    "Linear grows by equal differences; exponential grows by equal factors. A quantity growing exponentially eventually passes one growing linearly or quadratically."],
   examples:[
    {q:"Phone sales are S(t) = 500(1.2)<sup>t</sup>, t months after release. Find S(3), explain S(0), and give the domain and range for the first year.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 47A2024 · DOK 3",
     steps:["<span class='math'>S(3) = 500(1.2)<sup>3</sup> = 500(1.728) = 864</span> phones.","<span class='math'>S(0) = 500(1.2)<sup>0</sup> = 500</span>: the 500 phones sold at release (the initial value).","First year: domain <b>[0, 12]</b> months. S(12) = 500(1.2)<sup>12</sup> ≈ 4458, so the range is about <b>[500, 4458]</b>."]},
    {q:"Sales are modeled by S(t) = 2<sup>t</sup>. Describe how each strategy changes the graph: 2<sup>t</sup> + 3, 3·2<sup>t</sup>, and 0.5·2<sup>t</sup>.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 50A2024, Part B · DOK 3",
     steps:["<span class='math'>2<sup>t</sup> + 3</span>: every output goes up 3. The graph shifts up 3, the starting value becomes 4, and the asymptote moves from y = 0 to y = 3.","<span class='math'>3·2<sup>t</sup></span>: every output triples. The starting value becomes 3 and the graph rises more steeply.","<span class='math'>0.5·2<sup>t</sup></span>: every output is halved. The starting value becomes 0.5 and the graph is flatter."]},
    {q:"Compare S(t) = 2<sup>t</sup> with E(t) = t<sup>2</sup>. Which produces more units over time?", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 50A2024, Part C",
     steps:["t = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 → 2<sup>t</sup>: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64. t<sup>2</sup>: 0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36.","They tie at t = 2 and t = 4, and the quadratic is ahead only at t = 3. After t = 4 the exponential leads for good. Exponential eventually exceeds quadratic."]}],
   mis:[
    {t:"Treating an exponential graph as linear.", d:"From (0, 2) to (1, 2.4) the cost went up 0.4. That doesn't mean it adds 0.4 every year. Divide: 2.4 ÷ 2 = 1.2, a 20% increase each year."},
    {t:"Picking a decay factor for a graph that rises.", d:"A base less than 1 (like 0.87) makes the graph go down. A rising graph needs b &gt; 1."},
    {t:"Using the wrong point as the initial value.", d:"The initial value is the output at x = 0, not at x = 1."}],
   items:["S14","IB47","IB52"]}
 ]},

 {id:"par", name:"Patterning & Algebraic Reasoning", short:"Equations", pts:19,
  intro:"19 of 58 points. Linear inequalities and systems, quadratic expressions and equations, and exponential expressions and equations.",
  standards:[
  {code:"A.PAR.4", title:"Linear inequalities and systems of inequalities", unit:"Unit 2",
   text:"Create, analyze, and solve linear inequalities in two variables and systems of linear inequalities to model real-life phenomena.",
   keys:[
    "Translate the words: <b>at most / no more than</b> → ≤. <b>At least / no less than</b> → ≥. <b>Below / less than</b> → &lt;. <b>More than</b> → &gt;.",
    "Build the left side as (cost of one) × (how many) + (cost of other) × (how many), then compare to the limit.",
    "Graphing: ≤ and ≥ use a <b>solid</b> boundary line (points on the line count). &lt; and &gt; use a <b>dashed</b> line. The solution set is a half-plane: shade the side that makes the inequality true. Test a point like (0, 0) to decide.",
    "A <b>system</b> of inequalities is solved where the shaded regions overlap. In real situations, add constraints like x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0, because you can't buy negative tickets.",
    "A point is <b>possible</b> if it makes every inequality true and <b>not possible</b> if it breaks any of them."],
   examples:[
    {q:"Mrs. Parsons must keep a college tour below $6000. It costs $250 per student and $275 per adult. Write an inequality.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 09A2024 · DOK 2",
     steps:["Let s = students and a = adults.","Total cost: 250s + 275a. \"Below\" means strictly less than.","<b>250s + 275a &lt; 6000</b>"]},
    {q:"Carter needs at least $250. He makes $25 per grass cut (G) and $15 per trash run (T). Write an inequality.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 11A2024 · DOK 2",
     steps:["Money earned: 25G + 15T. \"At least\" means ≥.","<b>25G + 15T ≥ 250</b>"]},
    {q:"Esmerelda's family buys cinnamon rolls (r) at $3.75 and cookies (c) at $1.25 with $40, and must buy at least 16 items. Write and interpret the system.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 16A2024 · DOK 3",
     steps:["Budget: <span class='math'>3.75r + 1.25c ≤ 40</span>. Count: <span class='math'>r + c ≥ 16</span>. Also r ≥ 0 and c ≥ 0.","Both boundary lines are solid because both use ≤ or ≥.","Test (4, 12): 3.75(4) + 1.25(12) = 15 + 15 = 30 ≤ 40 ✓, and 4 + 12 = 16 ≥ 16 ✓. Possible.","Every whole-number point in the overlap is a combination they can afford that also meets the 16-item minimum."]},
    {q:"Theo has a $60 gift card. Popcorn is $5.50 and candy is $3.75. He wants at least 8 items. Set up the system.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 15A2024 · DOK 2",
     steps:["Let p = popcorn, c = candy.","<span class='math'>5.50p + 3.75c ≤ 60</span> (spending) and <span class='math'>p + c ≥ 8</span> (items), with p ≥ 0, c ≥ 0."]}],
   mis:[
    {t:"Reversing \"at least\" and \"at most.\"", d:"\"At least 16 items\" means 16 or more: ≥. \"At most $750\" means $750 or less: ≤."},
    {t:"Rounding a maximum up.", d:"If the math says s ≤ 25.7, the most students that fit the budget is 25. Rounding to 26 breaks the budget."},
    {t:"Solid vs. dashed lines.", d:"A point exactly on a ≤ boundary is a solution. Spending exactly $40 of $40 is allowed."}],
   items:["IB10","IB14B","IB16C"]},

  {code:"A.PAR.6", title:"Quadratic expressions and equations", unit:"Unit 4",
   text:"Build quadratic expressions and equations to represent and model real-life phenomena; solve quadratic equations in mathematically applicable situations.",
   keys:[
    "Parts of an expression: in <span class='math'>2x<sup>2</sup> + 15x + 18</span>, the terms are 2x<sup>2</sup>, 15x, and 18. The leading coefficient is 2 and the constant is 18. The degree is 2.",
    "Multiply binomials by distributing every term: <span class='math'>(x + 6)(2x + 3) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 3x + 12x + 18 = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 15x + 18</span>.",
    "Add or subtract polynomials by combining <b>like terms</b> only: x<sup>2</sup> with x<sup>2</sup>, x with x, constants with constants.",
    "Pick a solving method that fits the equation: inspection or square roots for <span class='math'>x<sup>2</sup> = p</span>; factoring; completing the square into <span class='math'>(x − p)<sup>2</sup> = q</span>; or the quadratic formula <span class='math'>x = (−b ± √(b<sup>2</sup> − 4ac)) / (2a)</span>.",
    "Area of a rectangle = length × width. If one side is written in terms of the other, you get a quadratic.",
    "Check your solutions in context. A negative length or a negative time is not possible, so reject it."],
   examples:[
    {q:"A poster's length is 2 inches more than twice its width. Its area is 112 in<sup>2</sup>. Find the dimensions.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 29A2024, Parts A and B · DOK 3",
     steps:["Width w, length 2w + 2. Area: <span class='math'>w(2w + 2) = 112</span>.","<span class='math'>2w<sup>2</sup> + 2w − 112 = 0</span> → divide by 2 → <span class='math'>w<sup>2</sup> + w − 56 = 0</span>.","Factor: <span class='math'>(w + 8)(w − 7) = 0</span> → w = 7 or w = −8. A width can't be negative.","<b>7 in wide, 16 in long.</b> Check: 7 × 16 = 112 ✓"]},
    {q:"Kim has 100 yards of fence for 3 sides of a pen against a barn: 2x + y = 100 and A = xy. Write the area as a quadratic and find the largest area.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 23A2024 · DOK 3",
     steps:["Solve the fence equation for y: y = 100 − 2x.","<span class='math'>A = x(100 − 2x) = −2x<sup>2</sup> + 100x</span>.","The vertex gives the maximum: <span class='math'>x = −100 / (2·(−2)) = 25</span>, so y = 100 − 50 = 50.","<b>25 yd by 50 yd, area 1250 yd<sup>2</sup>.</b>"]},
    {q:"Jaquaya's two boxes have areas (x)(2x + 5) and (3x)(x + 4). Find the combined area.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 30A2024 · DOK 2",
     steps:["<span class='math'>x(2x + 5) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 5x</span>.","<span class='math'>3x(x + 4) = 3x<sup>2</sup> + 12x</span>.","Combine like terms: <b>5x<sup>2</sup> + 17x</b>."]}],
   mis:[
    {t:"Dividing only part of the quadratic formula by 2a.", d:"x = (−6 ± √8) / 2 means divide both −6 and √8 by 2: −3 ± √2. Dividing only −6 gives −3 ± 2√2, which is wrong."},
    {t:"Combining unlike terms.", d:"6x + 7x = 13x, but 13x and 12 cannot be added. 2x<sup>2</sup> + 13x + 12 is fully simplified."},
    {t:"Multiplying when you should be factoring.", d:"If the area is x<sup>2</sup> + 6x, pull out the common factor: x(x + 6). Length x and width x<sup>2</sup> + 6 would give x<sup>3</sup> + 6x."},
    {t:"Keeping a negative solution for a length.", d:"Both roots solve the equation, but only the positive one can be a measurement."}],
   items:["S04","S07","S08","IB28","IB29C","IB31"]},

  {code:"A.PAR.8", title:"Exponential expressions and equations", unit:"Unit 5",
   text:"Create and analyze exponential expressions and equations to represent and model real-life phenomena; solve exponential equations in mathematically applicable situations.",
   keys:[
    "Exponential equation on your formula sheet: <span class='math'>y = a·b<sup>x</sup></span>. Compound interest: <span class='math'>A = P(1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup></span>.",
    "Growth: <span class='math'>y = a(1 + r)<sup>t</sup></span>. Decay: <span class='math'>y = a(1 − r)<sup>t</sup></span>. Here r is the rate as a decimal.",
    "To find the rate from a table with equal steps, divide each output by the one before. A constant ratio means exponential. A constant difference means linear.",
    "Situations with \"a percent per year\" (appreciates 17%, depreciates 2.5%, grows 3.4%) are exponential. \"A fixed amount per unit\" ($0.20 per song, 2.5 inches per hour) is linear.",
    "Check whether an input makes sense in context. A negative input means time before the starting point."],
   examples:[
    {q:"A car costs $52,350 and loses 5% of its value each year. Write P(t) and find the values for years 1 to 5.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 41A2024 · DOK 2",
     steps:["Decay rate 5% → factor 1 − 0.05 = 0.95. <span class='math'>P(t) = 52350(0.95)<sup>t</sup></span>.","Year 1: $49,732.50. Year 2: $47,245.88. Year 3: $44,883.58. Year 4: $42,639.40. Year 5: $40,507.43."]},
    {q:"Cells: 3 on day 1, 9 on day 2, 27 on day 3. Write an equation and find day 15.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 39A2024 · DOK 1",
     steps:["Each day multiplies by 3 (9 ÷ 3 = 3, 27 ÷ 9 = 3), and day n has 3<sup>n</sup> cells: <span class='math'>y = 3<sup>n</sup></span>.","Day 15: <span class='math'>3<sup>15</sup> = 14,348,907</span> cells."]},
    {q:"Bacteria: B(t) = 100·2<sup>t</sup>. Calculate B(−2). Does it mean anything?", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 45A2024 · DOK 2",
     steps:["<span class='math'>B(−2) = 100·2<sup>−2</sup> = 100 · 1/4 = 25</span>.","It means 25 bacteria 2 hours before the 100 were counted, if the culture was already growing at the same rate. If the culture started at t = 0, it has no meaning."]}],
   mis:[
    {t:"Using the rate instead of the factor.", d:"A 10% increase multiplies by 1.10, not 0.10. A 5% decrease multiplies by 0.95, not 0.05."},
    {t:"Reading the factor as the rate.", d:"If each year is 1.1 times the last, the rate is 1.1 − 1 = 0.1 = 10%."},
    {t:"Calling every rate situation exponential.", d:"Melting 2.5 inches per hour is a constant amount each hour, so it is linear."}],
   items:["S05","IB40","IB43","IB44"]}
 ]},

 {id:"gsr", name:"Geometric & Spatial Reasoning", short:"Geometry", pts:6,
  intro:"6 of 58 points. Distance, midpoint, slope, area, and perimeter on the coordinate plane.",
  standards:[
  {code:"A.GSR.3", title:"Distance, midpoint, slope, area, and perimeter", unit:"Unit 8",
   text:"Solve problems involving distance, midpoint, slope, area, and perimeter to model and explain real-life phenomena.",
   keys:[
    "Distance: <span class='math'>d = √((x<sub>2</sub> − x<sub>1</sub>)<sup>2</sup> + (y<sub>2</sub> − y<sub>1</sub>)<sup>2</sup>)</span>. Midpoint: <span class='math'>((x<sub>1</sub> + x<sub>2</sub>)/2, (y<sub>1</sub> + y<sub>2</sub>)/2)</span>. Both are on your formula sheet.",
    "Parallel lines have <b>equal</b> slopes. Perpendicular lines have slopes that are <b>opposite reciprocals</b> (like −2 and 1/2); their product is −1.",
    "Triangle area = ½ × base × height, where base and height are perpendicular. Parallelogram area = base × height.",
    "Perimeter = the sum of all side lengths. On a grid, find slanted side lengths with the distance formula.",
    "To prove a right angle on the coordinate plane, show two sides have perpendicular slopes."],
   examples:[
    {q:"A garden has vertices A(8, 2), B(10, 6), C(12, 2). Find the slope of BC and the line perpendicular to BC through (8, 2). Then find the perimeter.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 59A2024, Parts A and B · DOK 3",
     steps:["Slope of BC: <span class='math'>(2 − 6) / (12 − 10) = −4/2 = −2</span>.","Perpendicular slope: 1/2. Through (8, 2): <span class='math'>y − 2 = ½(x − 8)</span> → <b>y = ½x − 2</b>.","AB = √(2<sup>2</sup> + 4<sup>2</sup>) = √20 ≈ 4.47. BC = √20 ≈ 4.47. AC = 12 − 8 = 4.","Perimeter ≈ 4.47 + 4.47 + 4 = <b>12.94 units</b>."]},
    {q:"The playground is 6 units by 4 units. How much larger is it than the garden?", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 59A2024, Part C",
     steps:["Playground: 6 × 4 = 24 square units.","Garden: base AC = 4, height from B down to y = 2 is 4. Area = ½(4)(4) = 8.","24 − 8 = <b>16 square units larger</b>."]}],
   mis:[
    {t:"Forgetting the ½ in triangle area.", d:"Base × height gives the rectangle around the triangle. The triangle is half of that."},
    {t:"Using a non-perpendicular side as the height.", d:"Base and height must meet at a right angle. Check slopes first."},
    {t:"Subtracting coordinates in mismatched order.", d:"In the slope formula, if you start with point 2 on top, start with point 2 on the bottom too."}],
   items:["S09","IB57D","IB57M","IB60"]}
 ]},

 {id:"nr", name:"Numerical Reasoning", short:"Numbers", pts:6,
  intro:"6 of 58 points. Rational and irrational numbers, and rewriting square roots and cube roots.",
  standards:[
  {code:"A.NR.5", title:"Rational and irrational numbers; radicals", unit:"Unit 3",
   text:"Investigate rational and irrational numbers and rewrite expressions involving square roots and cube roots.",
   keys:[
    "A <b>rational</b> number can be written as a fraction of integers (4/7, −3, 0.25, √25 = 5). An <b>irrational</b> number can't (√2, √5, π). Its decimal never ends or repeats.",
    "A square root of a perfect square is rational: √25 = 5, √225 = 15. A square root of a non-perfect square is irrational: √200 = 10√2 ≈ 14.14.",
    "Simplify by pulling out the largest perfect-square factor: <span class='math'>√108 = √(36 · 3) = 6√3</span>. Cube roots pull out perfect cubes: <span class='math'>∛64 = 4</span>.",
    "Add or subtract only like radicals: <span class='math'>2√3 + 6√3 = 8√3</span>. Multiply: <span class='math'>√a · √b = √(ab)</span>.",
    "The rules: rational + rational = rational. Rational × rational = rational. Rational + irrational = <b>irrational</b>. Nonzero rational × irrational = <b>irrational</b>. Irrational + irrational can go either way (3√5 + √7 is irrational, but √2 + (−√2) = 0)."],
   examples:[
    {q:"Piece B is √108 feet long. Which measurements are equivalent: 6√3, 12√3, 3√12, 9√3, 3√9?", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 17A2024 · DOK 2",
     steps:["√108 = √(36 · 3) = 6√3.","3√12 = 3 · √(4 · 3) = 3 · 2√3 = 6√3. Also equivalent.","12√3, 9√3, and 3√9 = 9 are not equal to 6√3.","<b>6√3 and 3√12.</b>"]},
    {q:"A square has side length √8. Is its perimeter rational or irrational?", src:"Algebra C&C Standards, A.NR.5.2 example",
     steps:["Perimeter = 4√8 = 4 · 2√2 = 8√2.","8 is a nonzero rational and √2 is irrational. Their product is <b>irrational</b>."]},
    {q:"Two possible tower heights: 45 m and √200 m. Classify each.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 21A2024, Part A · DOK 3",
     steps:["45 = 45/1, a ratio of integers: <b>rational</b>.","√200 = √(100 · 2) = 10√2 ≈ 14.14, and 200 is not a perfect square: <b>irrational</b>.","A builder can measure 45 m exactly. 10√2 m can only be approximated, so 45 m is the more practical height."]}],
   mis:[
    {t:"\"Anything with a √ is irrational.\"", d:"√25 = 5 and √225 = 15 are rational because 25 and 225 are perfect squares."},
    {t:"Calling a fraction irrational.", d:"4/7 is a ratio of integers, so it is rational even though its decimal looks messy."},
    {t:"\"Two irrationals always add to an irrational.\"", d:"Not always: √2 + (−√2) = 0. The rule that always works is rational + irrational = irrational."},
    {t:"Flipping the number outside and inside the root.", d:"3√12 means 3 times √12 = 6√3. It is not the same as 12√3."}],
   items:["S03","IB17","IB22","IB21"]}
 ]},

 {id:"dsr", name:"Data & Statistical Reasoning", short:"Data", pts:6,
  intro:"6 of 58 points. Comparing data sets with center and spread, outliers, scatter plots, lines of best fit, and correlation.",
  standards:[
  {code:"A.DSR.10", title:"Univariate and bivariate data", unit:"Unit 7",
   text:"Collect, analyze, and interpret univariate quantitative data to answer statistical investigative questions that compare groups to solve real-life problems; represent bivariate data on a scatter plot and fit a function to the data to answer statistical questions and solve real-life problems.",
   keys:[
    "<b>Center:</b> mean (add all values, divide by how many) and median (middle value in order). <b>Spread:</b> range (max − min), interquartile range <span class='math'>IQR = Q<sub>3</sub> − Q<sub>1</sub></span>, and standard deviation (the typical distance of values from the mean).",
    "Standard deviation (formula sheet): find each value's distance from the mean, square it, add them up, divide by the number of values, then take the square root. Bigger SD = more spread out.",
    "<b>Outliers</b> are more than 1.5 × IQR below Q<sub>1</sub> or above Q<sub>3</sub>. Example: for {5, 7, 8, 10, 11, 12, 30}, Q<sub>1</sub> = 7 and Q<sub>3</sub> = 12, so IQR = 5 and the upper fence is 12 + 1.5(5) = 19.5. 30 is an outlier.",
    "Outliers pull the mean toward them but barely move the median. For skewed data or data with outliers, compare medians and IQRs. For symmetric data, mean and SD work well.",
    "<b>Scatter plots:</b> describe direction (positive or negative), strength (how tightly points hug a line), and form (linear or curved). The correlation coefficient <i>r</i> is between −1 and 1. Close to 1 or −1 is strong; close to 0 is weak.",
    "In a line of best fit, the <b>slope</b> is the predicted change in y for each 1-unit increase in x, and the <b>intercept</b> is the predicted y when x = 0. Choose linear, quadratic, or exponential models by the shape of the graphed data.",
    "<b>Correlation is not causation.</b> Two variables can move together without one causing the other."],
   examples:[
    {q:"Class A: 62, 90, 42, 92, 88, 84, 91, 87, 97, 100. Class B: 75, 80, 78, 85, 90, 82, 88, 84, 81, 87. Compare the classes.", src:"GaDOE Item Bank 53A2024 · DOK 3",
     steps:["Means: Class A = 833 ÷ 10 = <b>83.3</b>. Class B = 830 ÷ 10 = <b>83.0</b>. Almost the same.","Standard deviations (formula sheet method): Class A ≈ <b>16.86</b>. Class B ≈ <b>4.45</b>.","Class A has one low score (42) and another at 62 that pull its mean down and spread it out. Its median is 89; Class B's is 83.","Class B performed more consistently. Class A's typical student scored higher (median 89), but its scores vary much more."]}],
   mis:[
    {t:"Judging strength by the sign of r.", d:"r = −0.95 is stronger than r = 0.6. The sign gives direction; the distance from 0 gives strength."},
    {t:"Using the mean when there is an outlier.", d:"One extreme value can drag the mean far from where most data sit. Use the median and IQR."},
    {t:"Assuming correlation proves cause.", d:"Students who sleep more may score higher, but that data alone doesn't prove sleep caused the scores."}],
   items:["S06","S15","IB53","IB56M","IB56I","IB56O"]}
 ]}
];

