 S07:{src:"Test-style item", stem:`<p>Luis is looking at two rectangles with side lengths given as algebraic expressions.</p><p><b>Part A.</b> The area of the first rectangle is <span class="math">x<sup>2</sup> + 6x</span>. Which could be the length and width of the rectangle?</p>`,
  type:"mc", opts:[["a","Length: x, Width: x + 6",""],["b","Length: x, Width: x<sup>2</sup> + 6","Did not factor correctly (product would be x³ + 6x)"],["c","Length: x<sup>2</sup>, Width: 6x","Treated the terms as factors (product would be 6x³)"],["d","Length: 2x, Width: 3","Product is 6x, not x² + 6x"]], ans:"a",
  why:"Factor out the common x: x<sup>2</sup> + 6x = x(x + 6). Check: x · (x + 6) = x<sup>2</sup> + 6x ✓."},
 S08:{src:"Test-style item", stem:`<p>Luis is looking at two rectangles with side lengths given as algebraic expressions.</p><p><b>Part B.</b> The area of the second rectangle is <span class="math">x<sup>2</sup> + 7x + 12</span>. What is the sum of the areas of the two rectangles?</p>`,
  type:"mc", opts:[["a","x<sup>2</sup> + 7x + 18","Treated 6x as a constant and dropped one x² term"],["b","2x<sup>2</sup> + 13x + 12",""],["c","2x<sup>2</sup> + 25x","Combined unlike terms (added the constant 12 to the x-terms)"],["d","2x<sup>2</sup> + 7x + 12","Dropped the 6x term from the first rectangle"]], ans:"b",
  why:"(x<sup>2</sup> + 6x) + (x<sup>2</sup> + 7x + 12). Combine like terms: x<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> = 2x<sup>2</sup>; 6x + 7x = 13x; constant 12. Sum: 2x<sup>2</sup> + 13x + 12."},
 S09:{src:"Test-style item", stem:`<p>Triangle ABC has vertices as shown.</p>${triangleFig()}<p>What is the area of the triangle?</p>`,
  type:"mc", opts:[["a","√72 square units","Did not use ½ · base · height with perpendicular sides"],["b","12 square units",""],["c","√288 square units","Did not use ½ · base · height with perpendicular sides"],["d","24 square units","Forgot the ½ in triangle area"]], ans:"b",
  why:"Slope AB = (1 − 4)/(4 − 1) = −1. Slope AC = (4 − 0)/(1 − (−3)) = 1. Opposite reciprocals, so angle A is a right angle and AB, AC are the base and height. AB = √(9 + 9) = 3√2. AC = √(16 + 16) = 4√2. Area = ½ · 3√2 · 4√2 = ½ · 24 = 12."},
 S10:{src:"Test-style item", stem:`<p><b>Part A.</b> Which situation could be modeled by a function with a domain of all positive integers?</p>`,
  type:"mc", opts:[["a","the distance a runner has moved during a race as a function of time since the race started","Treated a continuous input (time) as whole numbers"],["b","the amount of fish food required in a fish tank as a function of the number of fish in the tank",""],["c","the amount of power required to operate a computer as a function of the length of time the computer is on","Treated a continuous input (time) as whole numbers"],["d","the amount of water required by an animal as a function of the mass of the animal","Treated a continuous input (mass) as whole numbers"]], ans:"b",
